WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.339
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:11.420
I fell hopelessly in love 15 years ago.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.710
I was only 16 years old,
a junior in high school.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:21.000
But Stacey Carson was a mature
sophisticated 18 year old.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.980
And as far as I could tell,
she was absolutely perfect.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.930
Drop dead gorgeous,

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:32.970
incredibly kind, a ton of fun to
hang out with, and she had a way of

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.549
making everyone she talk to feel that they
were the most special person in the world.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.089
She had my heart.

00:00:40.089 --> 00:00:43.943
When the opportunity came to ask her out,
I seized the moment,

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:48.785
a friend called one night to tell me that
she had just split with her boyfriend.

00:00:48.785 --> 00:00:51.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I put on my oversized green suit

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.640
and tie and
I spit on way too much cologne.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.490
I grab the rose on my mom's flower vase.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:02.160
And I jumped into my brother's
red 1987 Chevrolet Camaro and

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.460
I sped down to the high school two blocks
away, arriving just in time to see Stacy

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.790
exiting the school after finishing
her after school dance rehearsals.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.920
I put my foot to the gas and
I sped across the parking lot and

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.950
I came to a screeching
halt in front of Stacy.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.175
No doubt she was terrified.

00:01:17.175 --> 00:01:18.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.900
I swung open that big heavy door
&gt;&gt; And jumped and slid across the hood and

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:22.830
I extended the rose.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.490
[LAUGH] And then, without even thinking,

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.975
the words that came out of my
mouth were will you marry me.

00:01:29.975 --> 00:01:34.990
[LAUGH]
I was shocked,

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.672
because this is not what I wanted to say.

00:01:36.672 --> 00:01:41.175
[LAUGH] I was even more surprised
when she actually said yes.

00:01:41.175 --> 00:01:44.220
[LAUGH] Because that's who she was.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.580
And then she laughed and
I laughed, we laughed together for

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.100
a while and then I asked her to attend the
Winter Ball with me, to which she obliged.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.250
I spent the next two weeks doing
everything I could to make sure I would be

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.260
the perfect date for her,
including taking dance lessons.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.180
because she loved dancing and
I was terrible at it.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.670
But that wasn't going to stop me.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.250
And the night of the dance came and
it was magical.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.330
We danced the night away.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.190
I was falling in love.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:17.060
Five years later, I asked
Stacy Carson that same big question.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.125
This time, on purpose.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:23.046
[LAUGH] And
she said this time,without laughing Yes.

00:02:23.046 --> 00:02:27.820
And on August 19,
2006 at 1:00 PM we were married.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.970
It was another magical moment.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:32.700
We were in love.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.650
Five years passed approaching
our 5th wedding anniversary.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.520
And a lot had changed.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.080
I was working 80+ hours a week for
a crazy consulting firm and

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.180
my wife was working even harder to take
care of our six month old baby boy Jake.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.950
We were both completely exhausted.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.930
I came home one night after
a regular day in the office,

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.660
2 AM, and I walked in to find my poor
wife sitting on the kitchen floor.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.480
With tears streaming down
her mascara stained face.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.730
She looked at me, she said, why aren't
you here for me when I need you?

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:12.720
And then, without even thinking,
the words that came out of my mouth were,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.539
I'm doing the best that I can for this
family, why aren't you more appreciative?

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.710
And I went to bed that night ashamed and

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.540
asking myself the question,
what happened to our hopeless love?

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.440
Is this a sign that it's not meant to be?

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.636
I found the answers to these
questions in the work of Dr.

00:03:34.636 --> 00:03:36.972
Jennifer Finlayson-Fife, a marriage and

00:03:36.972 --> 00:03:41.030
family counselor from the University
of Boston, from Boston University.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.720
In her work she teaches that
love goes through three phases.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.210
Phase 1, love without knowledge.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.680
This is the blissfully and

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.850
well phase that we all enjoy when
we're first falling in love.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.960
This is the phase when your
partner is always on your mind and

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.280
you're willing to do anything for
her or him.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.670
Including taking dance lessons.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.500
In this phase,
your brain is actually addicted to love.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.900
Your brain enjoys heightened
levels of neurotransmitters,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.850
adrenaline, dopamine, and serotonin,

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:20.770
all of which form this crazy cocktail
of love that leaves you feeling joy,

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:26.630
vulnerability and
complete love, or so it seems.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:31.548
But this phase will not last forever,
because eventually that heightened level

00:04:31.548 --> 00:04:36.720
of euphoria goes away and you enter
phase two, knowledge without love.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.595
This is the might as
well be room mates phase.

00:04:38.595 --> 00:04:43.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In this phase, reality sets in, and

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.270
you start to notice the small things, and
the big things about your partner, that

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:51.479
are failing to meet your expectations,
and you stop trying to impress.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.980
This is the phase you
realize that love is hard.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.380
This is where I was on that
fateful night at 2 AM.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.850
But the beautiful thing is that
love can be better than that.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.380
And those who choose to leave that
phase will enter phase three,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:10.670
knowledge with love.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.840
In this phase you still have
a full appreciation for

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.230
your partner's weaknesses and faults.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.340
You still know exactly how
your relationship is failing

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.970
to meet your needs, but
you choose not to dwell on them.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:28.350
Instead, you choose to focus
on improving yourself for

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.210
your partner because love is worth it.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.760
And when the two partners equally
commit to working on self improvement,

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.020
they can grow in to phase three.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.990
And at that point you can really enjoy
the fruits of long lasting love and

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:44.610
companionship.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:49.670
Stacy and
I have been married ten years this August.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:52.929
And in a few days we welcome our
third child into our family.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:55.370
I love her.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.495
And we are not always in phase three.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.590
But we know how to get there.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.570
And we know that it's worth it.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:07.010
And we're both committed to working
toward it every single day.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.520
So I want to leave you with a few
tips that I've learned through my ten

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.195
years of marriage.

00:06:12.195 --> 00:06:16.218
[LAUGH] Which doesn't make me an expert,
but also not a novice.

00:06:16.218 --> 00:06:20.785
[LAUGH] I like to consider
myself an apprentice of love.

00:06:20.785 --> 00:06:27.470
[LAUGH]
Tip number one, learn how to love.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:30.910
Dr. Gary Chapman in his
New York Times bestselling book,

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:35.950
The Five Love Languages teaches us that
people feel loved in different ways.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.250
Your love language may be physical touch.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.290
Whereas yours maybe words of affirmation.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:48.350
This distinction does not matter in
phase one but it does in phase two.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.050
I know that Stacy is
particularly happy and

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.260
feeling love when I speak a specific
dialect of physical touch.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.340
The daily foot massage.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:07.060
I must have been high on dopamine
the day I committed to that one.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.635
Advice number two Learn how to fight.

00:07:12.635 --> 00:07:17.500
Dr. John Gottman is the master at this.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:20.750
In his 30 plus year longitudinal study,
he came to two, I think,

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:21.826
relevant conclusions.

00:07:21.826 --> 00:07:26.750
One, greater than 60% of relationship
conflict is unresolvable.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.499
It's simply a difference of opinions.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:31.780
And number two,

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.960
the marital masters learned to
fight with a smile on their face.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:41.050
I know this is easy to say, and
I know it's a lot harder to do, trust me.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:42.570
But I also know that it's worth it.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:45.140
So next time you're in a fight,
don't try to be right.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.910
Try to understand your
partner's point of view.

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.050
Try to argue with compassion.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.420
Finally, advice number three,
learn how to forgive.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:59.560
Unfortunately, I have seen how
an unwillingness to forgive

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.460
can tear apart families.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:05.930
The longer you're with someone the more
likely it is that feelings will be hurt.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.030
That's just the reality of love.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:11.642
And if we let that hurt
feelings linger for too long,

00:08:11.642 --> 00:08:17.055
it will poison your ability to live every
day expressing those love languages,

00:08:17.055 --> 00:08:22.629
to live every day arguing with compassion,
that prevents you in enjoying the true

00:08:22.629 --> 00:08:28.280
joys of long lasting relationships
that I enjoy everyday with Stacy.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.270
So that's it, no big secret.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.000
That's what works for me.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.779
So next time you find yourself in a long
term relationship and things get rocky,

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:42.348
because trust me they will,
rather than asking is this meant to be,

00:08:42.348 --> 00:08:47.372
first try asking what more can I do and
then get to work because it's hard.

00:08:47.372 --> 00:08:50.167
But I can tell you that it's worth it.

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:50.808
Thank you.

00:08:50.808 --> 00:08:53.478
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:53.478 --> 00:08:58.409
[MUSIC]

